.TH SYSREF 1 "August 2026" "sysref 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sysref \- minimal local man-page context for AI agents
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sysref
.RI QUERY " [" SECTION ]
.br
.B sysref
.B \-s
.RI SECTION " " QUERY
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-source
.BR man " | " doc " | " headers " | " api " " QUERY
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-stats
.RI QUERY " [" SECTION ]
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-bench
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-mcp
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-http
.RI [ =ADDR ]
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-web
.RI [ =ADDR ]
.br
.B sysref
.B \-\-conf
.I PATH
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sysref
answers one question \(em "what is the exact syntax of
.I X
?" \(em by extracting the smallest useful fragment from the native man pages,
package documentation, or system headers already installed on the host, and
serves that fragment over MCP, HTTP, or the command line.
.PP
It is a single static C binary with no dependencies, no database, no index,
no embeddings, no AI summaries, and no network.  Native
.B man
is authoritative: sysref never re-implements man's discovery or rendering.
Extraction works on whole lines only, and results are budget-capped
(\(<= ~400 tokens by default) so replies stay small.
.PP
The command name of a query is matched against locally installed manual pages
through native man's own discovery (configured via
.IR MANPATH ,
.IR /etc/man_db.conf ,
and the per-section search paths).  A failed lookup never silently switches
documentation source.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-stats
Print the result followed by token metrics on standard error: page name,
full bytes, returned bytes, estimated full and returned tokens, and the
reduction percentage.
.TP
.BI \-\-source = TYPE
Explicitly select the documentation source:
.BR man " (default), " doc ", " headers ", or " api .
Forcibly selecting a source never falls back to another one.
.TP
.B \-\-mcp
Run as a Model Context Protocol (MCP) stdio server.  Speaks JSON-RPC over
standard input/output and exposes a single tool (see
.BR "MCP" " section)."
.TP
.BI \-\-http[ =ADDR ]
Run as a webMCP HTTP/JSON-RPC server.  ADDR defaults to
.IR 127.0.0.1:8931 .
Requires
.B APIKEY
to be defined in the config file (see
.BR "HTTP" " and " "FILES" " sections)."
.TP
.BI \-\-web[ =ADDR ]
Alias for
.BR \-\-http .
.TP
.BI \-\-conf = PATH
Read the configuration file from
.I PATH
instead of
.IR /etc/sysref/sysref.conf .
The config supplies the bearer key used by the webMCP HTTP server.
.TP
.B \-\-bench
Print a benchmark table of the built-in query corpus with page, byte, token,
and reduction measurements, then exit.
.TP
.BI \-s " SECTION"
Select a specific manual section.  A numeric section (for example
.B 2
for system calls) selects the man page in that section; an alphabetic value
is treated as a named section heading (for example
.BR examples ,
.BR exit\ status ).
.PP
A bare command query returns the SYNOPSIS plus a one-line
SECTIONS map (for example
.B "SECTIONS: NAME | SYNOPSIS | ..."
) for progressive disclosure.  When no section is selected and the name
exists in several manual sections, the reply names the others so the
default choice stays visible.  A query matching nothing returns the available
headings; option-like queries (starting with
.B \- )
additionally list the nearest real option names.  A miss in any source
names the other tools to try next.
.SH SOURCES
Four documentation sources are available; each is selected explicitly with
.B \-\-source
and never falls back to another one.
.TP
.B man
(default) Native manual pages, resolved and rendered by the system
.B man
command.  GNU man-db long options are used when the local man understands
them, otherwise BSD/macOS-style invocation is used automatically.
.TP
.B doc
Package documentation already installed under
.IR /usr/share/doc :
plain text, READMEs, FAQ/INTRO, .txt, .md, and .html files.  Query terms are
matched against filenames, headings, and exact terms in the text; the smallest
complete paragraph matching the most terms wins.
.TP
.B headers
System C/C++ headers, resolved via the active include root (the macOS SDK path
when running under
.BR xcrun ,
otherwise
.IR /usr/include ).
A query names a header first, optionally followed by symbols to find inside it
(for example
.BR "sys/socket connect" ).
Results are whole declaration blocks matching every symbol, budget capped.
.TP
.B api
C symbol declaration plus local API documentation. Query form is
.BR "[header.h] symbol"
(one C identifier, header optional). Headers are resolved as for
.B headers
under
.IR /usr/include " and " /usr/local/include
(SYSREF_INCLUDE_PATH override); documentation is matched locally under
.IR /usr/share/doc ", " /usr/share/info ", and " /usr/share/man
(SYSREF_DOC_PATH override) with same-package/version excerpts preferred.
Only quoted local text is evidence; argument descriptions, returns,
preconditions, side effects, errors and sample invocations not stated there
are unavailable. Preprocessor branches are retained, never evaluated as active
configuration. Package ownership is reported from local metadata only; no
installation is performed and no package is inferred.
.PP
A bare-symbol query needs no filename: every configured include root is
searched.
The reply is a lifecycle block: SYMBOL, declaration SOURCE with file and
line, HEADER with file and line, PACKAGE owner/version from local metadata
(or unknown), verbatim DOCUMENT excerpts with Info/HTML/compressed manuals
included and HTML tags stripped, then DOCUMENTATION availability,
ARGUMENTS availability, STATUS, and a Detailed-local-manual line that is
explicit when no manual exists. A signature alone never establishes argument
meaning; when documentation is unavailable the agent is told so rather than
left to infer from types. The declaration's kind is stated plainly:
function-like and object-like macros (which have no address to take),
plain functions, and any static/inline/extern keywords literally present.
Enclosing conditional-compilation guards are quoted verbatim where present,
never evaluated. Where a quoted manual states a documented
signature for the symbol, its parameter list is compared with the header's
own form position by position (PARAMETERS), so names present only in the
manual are distinguished from the header text without inventing any.
Search bounds (file count, read bytes, depth,
match and reply limits) are reported when reached: a no-match is not an
exhaustive absence claim. Internal function implementations are out of
scope: headers and manuals describe the API contract, not the algorithm.
.SH MCP
In MCP mode
.B sysref
uses the Model Context Protocol over stdio and exposes exactly three tools.
The server identifies as
.B sysref
and advertises its purpose on initialize as local Linux documentation,
manual pages and C API reference.
.PP
.B  man(query, section?)
.RS
Look up Linux manual pages, commands and options.
.B section
selects a man section or named section heading.
.B source
is legacy: it accepts
.BR man ", " doc ", " headers ", or " api
for compatibility, but new agents use the dedicated tools below.
.RE
.PP
.B  doc(query)
.RS
Find installed package documentation, guides and examples.
.RE
.PP
.B  api(symbol?, header?)
.RS
Find C declarations, API contracts and header ownership. Either argument
suffices: a bare symbol searches every configured include root, a bare
header reports its availability, and both together scope the lookup.
.RE
.PP
Roughly 95% or more of the full page bytes is removed per call: results are
small declaration blocks, sections, or option entries instead of complete
pages.
.SH HTTP
The webMCP HTTP server speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over HTTP
.RI ( sysref\ \-\-http
or
.RB \-\-web ).
Requests authenticate with a bearer key in the
.B Authorization
header.  The key is read from the configuration file; without it the server
refuses to start.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sysref grep
sysref "grep -r"
sysref "grep exit status"
sysref "ip link set"
sysref "gcc -fPIC"
sysref -s 2 open
sysref --source doc "sudo troubleshooting"
sysref --source headers "sys/socket connect"
sysref --source api "stdio.h printf"
sysref --stats "rsync --delete"
.fi
.PP
.B sysref grep
returns the SYNOPSIS of
.B grep(1)
plus a SECTIONS map.  Adding an option name
.RB ( "grep \-r" )
returns that option's entry.  A named section
.RB ( "grep exit status" )
returns the section body.  A numeric section
.RB ( "sysref\ \-s\ 2\ open" )
selects the system-call page
.BR open(2) .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
A result was produced.
.TP
.B 1
The lookup failed (for example, no match, missing page, or a malformed
configuration at webMCP startup).
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid (unknown option, missing query, or too many
arguments).
.SH SECURITY
The following behaviour is proven and covered by the test suite:
.PP
.TP
\(bu
Queries are not executed through a shell.
.TP
\(bu
External commands (native
.BR man ,
.BR xcrun ,
package helpers) are executed with argv-style execution, not through a shell.
.TP
\(bu
Injection cases are covered by tests.
.PP
This is a concrete statement about what the code does; it is not a universal
security guarantee against every possible input class.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sysref/sysref.conf
Configuration file for the webMCP HTTP server.  A single
.B APIKEY=
assignment is read; blank lines and lines beginning with \fB#\fR are ignored.
.TP
.I /usr/share/man
Typical native man page search path in addition to any site-local
.IR /usr/local/share/man ,
.IR MANPATH ,
and libc-man_db entries.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR man (1),
.BR man-db (5),
.BR groff (1)